### What is the Factory pattern?

The Factory pattern has three parts: a product interface, concrete products, and a factory that maps an input to the right product.

```java
package com.bank.service;

// Product interface
public interface NotificationChannel {
    void send(String to, String message);
}

// Concrete products
public class SmsChannel implements NotificationChannel {
    public void send(String to, String message) {
        System.out.println("SMS to " + to + ": " + message);
    }
}

public class EmailChannel implements NotificationChannel {
    public void send(String to, String message) {
        System.out.println("Email to " + to + ": " + message);
    }
}

public class PushChannel implements NotificationChannel {
    public void send(String to, String message) {
        System.out.println("Push to " + to + ": " + message);
    }
}

// Factory
public class ChannelFactory {
    public static NotificationChannel create(String type) {
        return switch (type) {
            case "SMS" -> new SmsChannel();
            case "EMAIL" -> new EmailChannel();
            case "PUSH" -> new PushChannel();
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown channel: " + type);
        };
    }
}
```

The caller writes `ChannelFactory.create("SMS")` and then `channel.send(...)`, never knowing the concrete class.

### What are the benefits of Factory?

- **Decoupling**: client code depends only on the interface and the factory, not concrete classes.
- **Single point of change**: creation logic lives in one method; adding a cache or parameter touches one place.
- **OCP**: adding a channel means adding a class and one factory branch. Existing code is untouched.
- **Polymorphism**: the factory returns the interface type, enabling clean polymorphic dispatch.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class AlertService {
    // Depends only on NotificationChannel interface
    public void sendAlert(String channel, String to, String message) {
        NotificationChannel ch = ChannelFactory.create(channel);
        ch.send(to, message);
    }
}
```

### What are common use cases?

- Choosing an implementation by configuration: pick an SMS provider per region.
- Abstracting creation in frameworks: Spring's bean container is a factory.
- Hiding complex or conditional construction: building a parser based on file extension.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class FactoryUseCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Customer preference determines the channel
        String preference = "SMS"; // from database
        NotificationChannel channel = ChannelFactory.create(preference);
        channel.send("+91-9876543210", "Your transfer of 50,000 INR is complete.");
    }
}
```

```text
SMS to +91-9876543210: Your transfer of 50,000 INR is complete.
```

### How does Factory look in modern Java?

Replace the switch with a `Map<String, Supplier<Product>>`:

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

public class ModernChannelFactory {
    private static final Map<String, Supplier<NotificationChannel>> REGISTRY = Map.of(
        "SMS", SmsChannel::new,
        "EMAIL", EmailChannel::new,
        "PUSH", PushChannel::new
    );

    public static NotificationChannel create(String type) {
        Supplier<NotificationChannel> supplier = REGISTRY.get(type);
        if (supplier == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + type);
        }
        return supplier.get();
    }
}
```

The map is trivially extensible: adding a channel is adding one entry.

### How does Factory work in Spring?

In Spring, injecting a `Map<String, NotificationChannel>` gives a factory for free:

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.util.Map;

@Service
public class SpringAlertService {
    private final Map<String, NotificationChannel> channels;

    public SpringAlertService(Map<String, NotificationChannel> channels) {
        this.channels = channels;
    }

    public void send(String channelName, String to, String message) {
        NotificationChannel ch = channels.get(channelName);
        if (ch == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + channelName);
        ch.send(to, message);
    }
}
```

Adding a new channel is adding a new `@Component` bean. No factory edit.

### What are common mistakes with Factory?

- Having the factory return concrete types instead of the interface, defeating the decoupling.
- Over-using Factory for objects that have no variation: a plain `new` is simpler.
- Putting business logic inside the factory instead of in the products.

### How does Factory support testing?

Because the factory returns the interface, tests can create a mock or stub:

```java
// In tests
NotificationChannel mockChannel = (to, msg) -> System.out.println("MOCK: " + msg);
// inject mockChannel instead of calling the factory
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The switch-based factory is fine for a handful of types; the Map-based factory scales.
- In Spring, the container itself is a factory. Understanding this makes the pattern feel natural.
- Factory Method (GoF) specifically uses a method that subclasses override; the term "factory" in daily use is broader.
- At National Bank of India, the alert factory was the fix for scattered `new SmsChannel()` calls that caused missed alerts when push notifications were added.
